<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz Kafka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kafka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz Kafka was a German-language Jewish writer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> whose works fuse realism and the fantastique, typically with isolated protagonists facing bizarre predicaments and incomprehensible bureaucratic powers; the term Kafkaesque comes from them. He trained as a lawyer and worked in legal and insurance jobs, publishing only a minority of his works in his life, among them the collections Contemplation (1912) and A Country Doctor (1919) and the novella The Metamorphosis (1915). He died of tuberculosis in 1924, aged 40. His friend Max Brod ignored his wish that his remaining works be destroyed and published them, including The Trial (1925) and The Castle (1926).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-kafka.jpg" alt="Franz Kafka" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Kafka,_1923.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Franz Kafka was born near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-town-square-prague">Old Town Square</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, then part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, into a German-speaking middle-class Ashkenazi Jewish family. His father, Hermann Kafka, came to Prague in the 1870s and opened a store selling haberdashery and ladies' accessories, using a jackdaw (kavka in Czech) as its logo. Franz was the eldest of six children; his two brothers died in infancy, and his three sisters are believed to have been murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><p>He attended the German boys' elementary school from 1889 to 1893 and then the Altstädter Deutsches Gymnasium, where he also studied Czech for eight years, though he never considered himself fluent. He completed his Matura in 1901. As a teenager he declared himself an atheist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kafka was awarded the degree of Doctor of Law on 18 June 1906. On 1 November 1907 he joined the insurance company Assicurazioni Generali, but its working day, from 08:00 until 18:00, left little room for writing, and he resigned on 15 July 1908. Two weeks later he joined the Worker's Accident Insurance Institute for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, where he assessed compensation for injuries to industrial workers.</p><p>His earliest published work was eight stories printed in 1908 in the first issue of the journal Hyperion, later collected as Contemplation. He began his first novel in 1912; left unfinished, it was published by Max Brod after his death as Amerika. He met Felice Bauer on 13 August 1912, became engaged to several women, and never married.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kafka's laryngeal tuberculosis worsened, and in March 1924 he returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. He died on 3 June 1924 at a sanatorium in Kierling, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, apparently of starvation, since the condition of his throat made eating too painful. He had burned around 90 per cent of his work, and none of his full-length novels was finished. Brod ignored his request to destroy the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and arranged the publication of the three novels between 1924 and 1927.</p><p>Edwin and Willa Muir translated Das Schloss as The Castle in 1930. Writers who have acknowledged his influence include Gabriel García Márquez and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-kundera">Milan Kundera</a>, and W. H. Auden called him the Dante of the twentieth century. The word Kafkaesque describes situations in which bureaucracies overpower people in a nightmarish, disorienting setting.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franz Kafka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
